#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128994 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0559f0c-a1ca-5ba8-a02e-71d584273958" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/id #uuid "7caffa88-776d-52e1-8b81-2ebf98aedebc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6122" :question/problem "Let $R = [-1,4]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since $m_{ij}=M_{ij}=3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}3\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =3({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =3(4-(-1))(2-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=15 & \\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 15=U(f,P),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b9d2e98-8007-51ec-bfb6-cb9b48029a2a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using the Mean Value of X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "3464909c-5b04-5cb1-96c9-a409fa105bb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9494" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\\, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute $\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = (x_m^2 - x_0^2)(y_n-y_0)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that $\\bar{x}_i = \\dfrac 1 2 (x_{i-1}+x_i)$ will always lie between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i,$ and similarly $\\bar{y}_j = \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1}+y_j)$ will always lie between $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1} + y_j) \\leq x_{i} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j},\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + 2y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\leq x_{i} + 2y_{j}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j$ and applying the double summation gives\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\right]\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P).\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(y_{j-1} + y_j\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P).\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})(3-1)=4 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({3}^{2}-{1}^{2})(2-0)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 4+16\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 20\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/id #uuid "e75f2d8e-c165-5a24-942b-7326d7f11f0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9497" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,1]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 12-x-y.$ Further, let \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle I = \\iint\\limits_{R} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm dA\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\:\\leq I \\:\\leq U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums. Write down the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $U(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions $R_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\nM_{ij} = f(x_{i-1}, y_{j-1}) = 12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1}\n$$\n\nand the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0559f0c-a1ca-5ba8-a02e-71d584273958" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a021067-5b6e-57e1-9bbc-fac8f8980bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126027} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5ff80f-8f65-58dd-a9f2-7617ef31754f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126028} {:answer/id #uuid "d7493afc-707e-5a64-b9ef-2d31031fb02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126029} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f03156-e912-5b64-983b-1996e06a1ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126030} {:answer/id #uuid "61f6dd95-ed3d-5f2a-95a1-67579e58592c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5ff80f-8f65-58dd-a9f2-7617ef31754f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126028} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bfbbf1a-e710-5976-acf5-481a4e512358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e2508fb-892a-523d-953d-53a729ceb055" :question/math-academy-id "q-123482" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=5$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}5\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =5({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =5(4-2)(5-1) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=40\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 40=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=40.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3341f356-f3ff-58b8-ad69-453a1f95d36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566462} {:answer/id #uuid "4f462d0e-b4c3-5c41-a919-292a21a0df82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566513} {:answer/id #uuid "aacac42d-b1be-5602-bedb-2f4c69f31e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the options is correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566702} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c419a4-5a67-54d6-888f-96fb515aa062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566787} {:answer/id #uuid "d70d7d3f-e229-5967-82c3-9d7d233d6f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{x}_{i}-{y}_{j-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3341f356-f3ff-58b8-ad69-453a1f95d36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566462} :answer-field/id #uuid "34398d63-6e1c-5068-b174-02c085d16b64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02febc61-94f2-5b4e-865b-483532db6a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-123738" :question/problem "Let $R=[-4,-1]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-x-y.$ Further, let \n\n$$\nI={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\,\\le I\\,\\le U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and \n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $U(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}=f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=10-{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}\n$$\n\nand the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(10-{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $10-{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5850cba3-8505-5490-af92-ff55d93ad7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-2{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566529} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd17b0a-f96f-5478-adb3-fca9bb8a8df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the options is correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566568} {:answer/id #uuid "757b9da1-118e-533a-9f87-773bf8421955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-2{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566585} {:answer/id #uuid "90aafbe6-4932-5490-9a56-ed4cbe177dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-2{x}_{i}^{2}-{y}_{j}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566645} {:answer/id #uuid "e1751e07-ab63-55d5-b441-84b4cc059fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-2{x}_{i}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757b9da1-118e-533a-9f87-773bf8421955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-2{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566585} :answer-field/id #uuid "b445ce38-a658-508d-aa3e-0d6849827d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7ace049-182d-5867-9256-197b885a5673" :question/math-academy-id "q-123739" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,2]\\times [3,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=25-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Further, let \n\n$$\nI={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\,\\le I\\,\\le U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and \n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $U(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=25-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=25-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}=f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=25-2{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}\n$$\n\nand the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(25-2{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $25-2{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8105fb-f4bc-5a6f-bfb3-ae21d2a232b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the options is correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566386} {:answer/id #uuid "76de735e-25f7-5f2a-9828-8a17a8c36678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{i}+3{y}_{j}+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566590} {:answer/id #uuid "cf45db66-4e82-5bf4-8693-7015a139d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{i}+3{y}_{j-1}+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566773} {:answer/id #uuid "d67a5fd5-949a-58d4-ad64-2dc249dc2ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{i-1}+3{y}_{j-1}+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566790} {:answer/id #uuid "f475323c-f196-5623-a711-90843ce4a095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{i-1}+3{y}_{j}+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d67a5fd5-949a-58d4-ad64-2dc249dc2ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{i-1}+3{y}_{j-1}+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566790} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eba9e2b-5161-56bf-affd-dc4a4f03fcd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8dc68c16-6345-555e-bdf6-2d7d630e9344" :question/math-academy-id "q-123743" :question/problem "Let $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=9x+3y+50.$ Further, let \n\n$$\nI={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\,\\le I\\,\\le U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $L(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=9x+3y+50$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=9x+3y+50$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}=f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=9{x}_{i-1}+3{y}_{j-1}+50\n$$\n\nand the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(9{x}_{i-1}+3{y}_{j-1}+50)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $9{x}_{i-1}+3{y}_{j-1}+50.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer